        content: `<section><h2 id="progressive-decentralization">Progressive decentralization</h2><div class="roadmap"><div class="roadmap-item" data-step="01"><h3>Core build</h3><p>The team owns product, security, and economic boundaries; forums and trial votes collect feedback.</p></div><div class="roadmap-item" data-step="02"><h3>Co-governance</h3><p>Gameplay, economy, security, and community councils govern selected budgets and parameters.</p></div><div class="roadmap-item" data-step="03"><h3>DAO execution</h3><p>Mature matters move through on-chain votes, timelocks, multisigs, and emergency review.</p></div></div></section><section><h2 id="treasury">Community treasury</h2><p>The treasury supports public content, developer tools, audits, education, competition, creators, and reserves. Spending states goals, milestones, owners, budgets, conflicts, and proof.</p></section><section><h2 id="proposal-process">Proposal process</h2><ol class="flow-list"><li><b>Discuss</b><span>Publish the problem, options, cost, and impact.</span></li><li><b>Temperature check</b><span>Use a low-cost non-binding vote.</span></li><li><b>Formal proposal</b><span>Complete legal, security, economic, and execution review.</span></li><li><b>Timelocked execution</b><span>Wait publicly before multisig or contract execution.</span></li><li><b>Review</b><span>Publish spending, outcomes, variance, and next steps.</span></li></ol></section><section><h2 id="governance-weight">Weight and safeguards</h2><p>Governance may combine node base weight, locked TUR, non-transferable contribution reputation, delegation, and account caps. Quadratic methods and council review may reduce whale control and conflicts.</p><div class="callout"><p>A DAO is not an instant substitute for team accountability. Authority moves only after product, tools, participation, and security meet public thresholds.</p></div></section>`
